tool_input.script:
export const meta = {
  name: 'order-scan-bot-1-3',
  description: 'Read-only independent analysis of order/position scan and count functions in bot 1 and bot 3',
  phases: [{ title: 'Phan tich', detail: '5 read-only agents, one per sub-question' }],
}

const COMMON = `Context: The project owner confirmed these MT5 bots are their own, the source was lost, and recovery is authorized per AGENTS.md. Task: read-only analysis of decompiled C (captured native code) to specify behavior precisely for a faithful MQL5 rewrite. Do NOT run shell commands, do NOT open MT5, do NOT create/edit any file, do NOT touch EX5 or licensing. Use only Read/Grep/Glob.

Repo root: C:\\Users\\NTD\\Desktop\\Bot Market Demo
Bot 1 C: dich-nguoc/khoi-phuc/bot-cua-toi-1/phan-tich/luot-20261023-data-v2/ma-trung-gian.c (code base address 0x23e5aa10000; RVA = address - base; e.g. FUN_23e5aa41f40 = RVA 0x31f40, DAT_23e5aa75dd0 = RVA 0x65dd0)
Bot 3 C: dich-nguoc/khoi-phuc/bot-cua-toi-3/phan-tich/luot-20261023-data/ma-trung-gian-day-du.c (code base 0x17156250000; FUN_1715628e490 = RVA 0x3e490)
Maps: dich-nguoc/khoi-phuc/ban-do-bot-1-3/luot-20261024-r8/bot-1.json and bot-3.json (function ranges, callers).
API meaning rules: names like MT5_OrdersTotal come from address labels in .../metadata/native-labels.tsv (bot 1: dich-nguoc/khoi-phuc/bot-cua-toi-1/phan-tich/luot-20261023-data-v2/metadata/native-labels.tsv; bot 3 similar path), produced from known-source probes (dich-nguoc/khoi-phuc/bot-cua-toi-13/phan-tich/ky-hieu-v4/native-labels.json; probe bodies in dich-nguoc/khoi-phuc/bot-cua-toi-13/bang-chung/doi-chung-api/probes.json; same-tester-build check in dich-nguoc/khoi-phuc/cong-cu/tao-ho-so-ma-chup.py). Numeric enum meanings: dich-nguoc/khoi-phuc/bot-cua-toi-13/bang-chung/doi-chung-api/enum-values.json. MT5_Dispatch opcode 2 = Print (dich-nguoc/khoi-phuc/ban-do-bot-1-3/luot-20261024-r8/api-calibration.json). Never infer meaning from FUN_/DAT_ placeholder names.
The C is a decompilation, not source: say where native bytes would be needed to settle a type or conversion.
Cite every claim as path:line (C line numbers) plus RVA. Be concise and precise. Mark anything not provable from the files as unknown. Do not claim the whole bot is complete.`

const SCHEMA = {
  type: 'object',
  properties: {
    summary: { type: 'string' },
    items: {
      type: 'array',
      items: {
        type: 'object',
        properties: {
          topic: { type: 'string' },
          finding: { type: 'string' },
          evidence: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'string' } },
          status: { type: 'string', enum: ['verified_from_C', 'verified_by_existing_test', 'inferred', 'unknown', 'contradiction'] },
        },
        required: ['topic', 'finding', 'evidence', 'status'],
      },
    },
    unknowns: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'string' } },
    required_tests: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'string' } },
  },
  required: ['summary', 'items', 'unknowns', 'required_tests'],
}

const TASKS = [
  {
    key: 'count',
    prompt: `Scope: Bot 1 RVA 0x31f40 (C lines 21233-21300) and RVA 0x323e0 (C lines 21401-21468); their call site in bot 1 RVA 0x18aa0 at C lines 13096-13110; and the bot 3 counterpart, which appears inlined in bot 3 RVA 0x20ef0 at C lines 17862-17965 (confirm bot 3 has no standalone equivalent by checking the bot 3 map, and that these inline loops are the counterpart). Reference: a structurally similar function, bot 1 RVA 0x3c670 (C 26734-26809), was reconstructed as Bot1NearbyPending in dich-nguoc/khoi-phuc/bot-cua-toi-1/source/MQL5/Include/bot-cua-toi-1/Bot1EntryLevels.mqh and compared by dich-nguoc/khoi-phuc/cong-cu/kiem-gia-vao-bot-1-3.py (result dich-nguoc/khoi-phuc/bot-cua-toi-1/bang-chung/kiem-gia-vao/entry-20261023-r3/comparison.json); say exactly which properties that test covers and which it does not, for transferring confidence to the count functions.
Determine precisely: loop start/end/direction and whether an out-of-range index is queried; handling of ticket 0; exact order of API calls with arguments and enum meaning; magic comparison (which global, its width, sign extension); symbol string comparison semantics including null vs empty pointer cases (string assign helper FUN_23e5aa105c0 at C 390-510, free helper FUN_23e5aa10000 at C 7-27); order type filter values; return type/value; every global written and the value it holds at return; short-circuit (which calls are skipped when an earlier filter fails); early break or not. At the call site: all gating conditions (incl. any account-wide OrdersTotal() check), what the count is compared with (global, type, operator), what is called next. For bot 3 inline loops list every difference from bot 1 and map equivalent globals bot1<->bot3.`,
  },
  {
    key: 'prune',
    prompt: `Scope: Bot 1 RVA 0x32ce0 (C 21675-21718) and bot 3 RVA 0x3e490 (C 26916-26959). Call sites: bot 1 RVA 0x18aa0 C 13795-13810 (indirect call through a function-pointer variable; the bot-1 map lists no callers for 0x32ce0 - explain why) and bot 3 RVA 0x20ef0 C 18519-18524.
Determine: which table is scanned (address, RVA, rows, row size, element type); row order; the inner loop bound (which API supplies it - verify the label by address in native-labels.tsv, e.g. compare with PositionsTotal's address) and which API is called per index; off-by-one; how a row counts as found (conversion of the stored value, comparison, role of ticket 0, early break or not); what is written when not found (both columns, order); every global written and its final value; number of API calls per invocation as a function of OrdersTotal() and 100 rows; the gate at the call site (which globals; find what DAT_23e5aa7779c / DAT_23e5aa77448 and bot 3 DAT_171562c47dc / DAT_171562c4488 are: search where the struct at DAT_23e5aa77790 / DAT_171562c47d0 is filled, e.g. MT5_TimeCurrent with a struct pointer, and how its fields are used elsewhere); under which earlier branches of 0x18aa0 / 0x20ef0 the call is never reached. Confirm whether the bot 1 and bot 3 versions are identical except addresses. The table at bot 1 RVA 0x65dd0 / bot 3 RVA 0x72e10 is described in dich-nguoc/khoi-phuc/PHAN-TICH-HANG-DOI-BOT-1-3.md (around line 20): check whether it is the same table.`,
  },
  {
    key: 'limit',
    prompt: `Scope: Bot 3 RVA 0x47330 (C 31575-31589) and its call sites in bot 3 RVA 0x20ef0 near C 18440-18471 and 19333-19364; bot 1 inline equivalents in RVA 0x18aa0 near C 13397-13430 and 13697-13730 (grep MT5_AccountInfoInteger with 0x2f in both files).
Determine: enum meaning of 0x2f; integer width/truncation of the limit and of each comparison as visible in C (flag where native bytes are needed); return value for limit 0, positive, negative, and when only the high 32 bits are nonzero; exact call order and short-circuit with the surrounding conditions (the volume-validation call before it, the price-distance checks after it, and which order type is then requested: second argument of FUN_23e5aa42dd0 / FUN_1715628e580); whether OrdersTotal here is account-wide or filtered; whether bot 1 (inline) and bot 3 (function) differ in semantics. List any other place in either bot that compares OrdersTotal() with an account limit.`,
  },
  {
    key: 'selection',
    prompt: `Scope: the shared ticket global and terminal order/position selection. Bot 1 global DAT_23e5aa777f8 (RVA 0x677f8), bot 3 DAT_171562c4838 (RVA 0x74838). Grep all references in both C files.
For each point below, trace FORWARD along control flow to determine whether later code, before the next fresh write/selection, reads the shared ticket global or calls property readers (MT5_OrderGetInteger/Double/String, MT5_PositionGetInteger/Double/String and Ref variants, per native-labels.tsv) that would depend on the selection left behind:
 - bot 1: after FUN_23e5aa41f40 (C 13100) and FUN_23e5aa423e0 (C 13104); after FUN_23e5aa42ce0 (indirect call at C 13810, near the end of RVA 0x18aa0) - find who calls 0x18aa0 (FUN_23e5aa28aa0) and what runs after it returns.
 - bot 3: after the inline count loops (C 17862-17965); after FUN_1715628e490 (C 18523) - find who calls 0x20ef0 (FUN_17156270ef0) and what runs next; and confirm FUN_17156297330 (C 31575-31589, called at C 18456/19349) selects nothing.
Also list which functions write the shared ticket from OrderGetTicket and which from PositionGetTicket (i.e. whether one variable serves both order and position scans), and give at most 5 clearest examples where a reader reads it after calling another function that may overwrite it. State clearly what is proven vs not checked.`,
  },
  {
    key: 'table',
    prompt: `Scope: the 100x2 double table at bot 1 RVA 0x65dd0..0x6640f (seen as DAT_23e5aa75dd0/75dd8 with positive offsets, or as DAT_23e5aa76410/76418 plus negative offsets from -0x640) and bot 3 RVA 0x72e10..0x7344f (DAT_171562c2e10/2e18, or DAT_171562c3450/3458 plus negative offsets). Careful: the neighbouring 100x3 double table at bot 1 RVA 0x65470 (0x960 bytes, ends at 0x65dd0; bot 3 0x724b0) can appear as DAT_23e5aa75dd0 plus NEGATIVE offsets with stride 0x18; and DAT_23e5aa76410/76418 (bot 3 DAT_171562c3450/3458) are also used as separate scalar globals (e.g. byte-flag compares with '\\x01'). Distinguish all of these by offset range and stride.
List every function (RVA, C lines) that reads or writes the 100x2 table, what goes into column 0 and column 1 (as shown in C: e.g. (double) of the bool returned by FUN_23e5aa42dd0, a position ticket converted to double, (double)(int)ticket, etc.), and how lookups compare (exact double ==, NaN handling, conversions). Use dich-nguoc/khoi-phuc/PHAN-TICH-HANG-DOI-BOT-1-3.md and dich-nguoc/khoi-phuc/bot-cua-toi-1/source/MQL5/Include/bot-cua-toi-1/Bot1PendingQueue.mqh as reference for the queue writer. Then state which kinds of rows the hourly cleanup (bot 1 RVA 0x32ce0, C 21675-21718; bot 3 RVA 0x3e490, C 26916-26959) keeps and which it clears, given that it scans indexes OrdersTotal()..0 with PositionGetTicket.`,
  },
]

phase('Phan tich')
const results = await parallel(TASKS.map(t => () =>
  agent(COMMON + '\n\n' + t.prompt, { label: 'scan:' + t.key, phase: 'Phan tich', schema: SCHEMA, effort: 'max' })
    .then(r => ({ key: t.key, result: r }))
))
return results.filter(Boolean)
